    "in downtown Chicago. The conference is planned to begin Friday mid- to late afternoon and end Sunday by early afternoon. Final timing and venue details will be confirmed here.": "no centro de Chicago. A conferência está planejada para começar na sexta-feira, do meio ao final da tarde, e terminar no domingo, no início da tarde. O horário final e os detalhes do local serão confirmados aqui.",
    "info@cureEDMD.org": "info@cureEDMD.org",
    "is Professor of Medicine and Pathology and Cell Biology at Columbia University and Attending Physician at New York-Presbyterian Hospital Columbia University Irving Medical Center. Dr. Worman received his BA from Cornell University and MD from the University of Chicago. He did an internship and residency in internal medicine at New York Hospital-Cornell Medical Center and a postdoctoral research fellowship in cell biology at Rockefeller University, where he started his research on the nuclear envelope.": "é professor de medicina e patologia e biologia celular na Universidade de Columbia e médico assistente no New York-Presbyterian Hospital Columbia University Irving Medical Center. Dr. Worman recebeu seu bacharelado pela Cornell University e MD pela University of Chicago. Fez estágio e residência em medicina interna no New York Hospital-Cornell Medical Center e pós-doutorado em biologia celular na Rockefeller University, onde iniciou suas pesquisas sobre o envelope nuclear.",
    "is a professor in the Meinig School of Biomedical Engineering and the Weill Institute for Cell and Molecular Biology at Cornell University. He was appointed as the Associate Director of the Meinig School of Biomedical Engineering in 2024. He obtained a Diplom Ingenieur degree in Mechanical Engineering in his native Germany and a Bachelor of Engineering degree from the Thayer School of Engineering at Dartmouth College. Subsequently, he completed his Ph.D. in Biological Engineering at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology studying subcellular biomechanics and mechanotransduction signaling with Drs. Roger Kamm and Richard T. Lee (Brigham and Women’s Hospital/Harvard Medical School). Following a brief postdoctoral fellowship in Dr. Lee’s laboratory, he started his faculty career at Harvard Medical School/Brigham and Women’s Hospital while also teaching in the Department of Biological Engineering at the Massachusetts Institute for Technology before joining Cornell University in 2011.": "é professor da Escola Meinig de Engenharia Biomédica e do Instituto Weill de Biologia Celular e Molecular da Universidade Cornell. Ele foi nomeado Diretor Associado da Escola Meinig de Engenharia Biomédica em 2024. Ele obteve o diploma Diplom Ingenieur em Engenharia Mecânica em sua Alemanha natal e o diploma de Bacharel em Engenharia pela Thayer School of Engineering no Dartmouth College. Posteriormente, ele completou seu doutorado. em Engenharia Biológica no Instituto de Tecnologia de Massachusetts, estudando biomecânica subcelular e sinalização de mecanotransdução com os Drs. Roger Kamm e Richard T. Lee (Brigham and Women’s Hospital/Harvard Medical School). Após uma breve bolsa de pós-doutorado no laboratório do Dr. Lee, ele iniciou sua carreira docente na Harvard Medical School/Brigham and Women’s Hospital, ao mesmo tempo em que lecionava no Departamento de Engenharia Biológica do Massachusetts Institute for Technology antes de ingressar na Cornell University em 2011.",
