    "is Professor of Medicine and Pathology and Cell Biology at Columbia University and Attending Physician at New York-Presbyterian Hospital Columbia University Irving Medical Center. Dr. Worman received his BA from Cornell University and MD from the University of Chicago. He did an internship and residency in internal medicine at New York Hospital-Cornell Medical Center and a postdoctoral research fellowship in cell biology at Rockefeller University, where he started his research on the nuclear envelope.": "è Professore di Medicina, Patologia e Biologia Cellulare presso la Columbia University e Medico curante presso il New York-Presbyterian Hospital Columbia University Irving Medical Center. Il Dr. Worman ha conseguito la laurea presso la Cornell University e la laurea in medicina presso l'Università di Chicago. Ha svolto uno stage e una specializzazione in medicina interna presso il New York Hospital-Cornell Medical Center e una borsa di ricerca post-dottorato in biologia cellulare presso la Rockefeller University, dove ha iniziato la sua ricerca sull'involucro nucleare.",
    "is a professor in the Meinig School of Biomedical Engineering and the Weill Institute for Cell and Molecular Biology at Cornell University. He was appointed as the Associate Director of the Meinig School of Biomedical Engineering in 2024. He obtained a Diplom Ingenieur degree in Mechanical Engineering in his native Germany and a Bachelor of Engineering degree from the Thayer School of Engineering at Dartmouth College. Subsequently, he completed his Ph.D. in Biological Engineering at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology studying subcellular biomechanics and mechanotransduction signaling with Drs. Roger Kamm and Richard T. Lee (Brigham and Women’s Hospital/Harvard Medical School). Following a brief postdoctoral fellowship in Dr. Lee’s laboratory, he started his faculty career at Harvard Medical School/Brigham and Women’s Hospital while also teaching in the Department of Biological Engineering at the Massachusetts Institute for Technology before joining Cornell University in 2011.": "è professore alla Meinig School of Biomedical Engineering e al Weill Institute for Cell and Molecular Biology presso la Cornell University. È stato nominato Direttore associato della Meinig School of Biomedical Engineering nel 2024. Ha conseguito un Diplom Ingenieur in Ingegneria Meccanica nella sua nativa Germania e una laurea in Ingegneria presso la Thayer School of Engineering del Dartmouth College. Successivamente, ha completato il suo dottorato di ricerca. in Ingegneria Biologica presso il Massachusetts Institute of Technology studiando biomeccanica subcellulare e segnalazione di meccanotrasduzione con i Drs. Roger Kamm e Richard T. Lee (Brigham and Women’s Hospital/Harvard Medical School). Dopo una breve borsa di studio post-dottorato nel laboratorio del Dr. Lee, ha iniziato la sua carriera universitaria presso la Harvard Medical School/Brigham and Women's Hospital, insegnando anche presso il Dipartimento di ingegneria biologica presso il Massachusetts Institute for Technology prima di unirsi alla Cornell University nel 2011.",
